---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Param from "../../../components/Param.astro";
import Measured from "../../../components/Measured.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="alignment"
  title="Aligning the frames"
  description="Deptheos's two aligners, ECC and grid search: which to use, how far each lets a frame move, and what happens to a frame that can't be aligned."
  hasLabs
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Aligning the frames</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Before stacking, Deptheos lines each frame up with the one before it, so the subject stays in place while
      the focus moves through it. There are two aligners, <strong>ECC</strong> (Enhanced Correlation
      Coefficient), the default, and the <strong>grid search</strong>, chosen for each stack under Alignment in
      the inspector.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="which">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Which one to use</h2>
      <p>
        Use <strong>ECC</strong> unless a frame moves further between shots than ECC allows:
        <Param path="constants.ecc.max_shift_pct" />&nbsp;% of the frame in shift,
        <Param path="constants.ecc.max_scale_pct" />&nbsp;% in size or
        <Param path="constants.ecc.max_rotation_deg" />° of rotation. A stack shot on a tripod or a
        focus rail moves far less than that from frame to frame, a fraction of a percent, and ECC finds that
        motion to a fraction of a pixel.
      </p>
      <p>Switch to the <strong>grid search</strong> when:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Frames jump between shots: a stack shot by hand, a rail that slipped, or large focus steps with a lens
          whose magnification changes a lot as it focuses. When ECC leaves frames out for moving too far, the
          notice after the stack says so. If the grid search still leaves frames out, raise its limits in
          Settings ▸ Alignment.
        </li>
        <li>
          The exposure changes through the stack. Turn on <em>Exposure compensation</em> in Settings ▸
          Alignment as well, so each frame is brought to the first frame's brightness.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="compare">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How the two compare</h2>
      <p>
        Both align each frame to the one before it and chain the steps together, so the last frame's position
        is the sum of every step before it. They differ in how they search for each step and how far they let
        a frame move.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="choose">
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>ECC</th><th>Grid search</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th>How it searches</th>
            <td>Starts from no motion and improves its guess step by step, on small copies of the frames first, then larger ones</td>
            <td>Scores a spread of guesses across its limits, then finer ones around the best</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>What it finds</th>
            <td class="best">Shift, rotation, size, stretch and slant (a full affine transform), to a fraction of a pixel</td>
            <td>Shift, rotation and size only. Stretch and slant are small in a focus bracket, but grow toward the corners, where missing them can soften fine detail</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>How far a frame can move from the one before</th>
            <td class="weak">
              <Param path="constants.ecc.max_shift_pct" />&nbsp;% of the frame's width or height in
              shift, <Param path="constants.ecc.max_scale_pct" />&nbsp;% in size,
              <Param path="constants.ecc.max_rotation_deg" />° of rotation. Fixed; a frame that moves
              more is left out
            </td>
            <td class="best">
              <Param path="defaults.grid.horizontal_pct" />&nbsp;% of the frame's longer side in
              shift, <Param path="defaults.grid.magnification_pct" />&nbsp;% in size,
              <Param path="defaults.grid.rotation_deg" />° of rotation by default; Settings raise
              these up to <Param path="ranges.grid.shift_pct.max" />&nbsp;%,
              <Param path="ranges.grid.magnification_pct.max" />&nbsp;% and
              <Param path="ranges.grid.rotation_deg.max" />°. It can also follow a match past the
              edge of its search
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Brightness changing between frames</th>
            <td>A change across the whole frame is ignored; uneven changes, such as light that moved, are not</td>
            <td>Allowed for while it searches. <em>Exposure compensation</em> also applies it to the stack, for stacks shot at varying exposure</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>A frame it can't align</th>
            <td>
              Left out when the frames correlate poorly (below
              <Param path="constants.ecc.min_correlation" />, where 1 is a perfect match) or overlap on
              less than a quarter of the frame, or when the motion exceeds its limits
            </td>
            <td>
              Left out when its best match is hardly better than two unrelated pictures would give. After a
              featureless frame it has nothing to judge by and keeps the frame, so check the result
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>What it needs</th>
            <td>Detail in the frames; a plain, smooth subject gives it little to follow</td>
            <td>The same</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Settings</th>
            <td>None</td>
            <td>Its limits and <em>Exposure compensation</em>, in Settings ▸ Alignment</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Time</th>
            <td>
              <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.runs.pyramid.ecc.seconds" digits={1} />&nbsp;s
              for <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.stack.frames" /> JPEGs, aligned and
              stacked with the pyramid
            </td>
            <td>
              <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.runs.pyramid.grid.seconds" digits={1} />&nbsp;s
              for the same, at its default limits; wider limits take longer
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="meta">
        Times on an <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.label" />, with the circuit-board
        sample stack at <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.stack.megapixels" digits={0} /> MP.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="settings">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Frames left out, the stack's direction, and the crop</h2>
      <h3>A frame that can't be aligned</h3>
      <p>
        A frame either aligner can't place is left out, and the stack is made without it. The next frame is
        aligned to the last frame that did align, so one bad frame does not throw off the rest. After the stack,
        a notice names each frame left out and why, and the Library shows the render's frame count as, for
        example, "44 of 46". If many frames are left out for moving too far, try the grid search.
      </p>
      <h3>Which end of the stack leads</h3>
      <p>
        The frame the others are aligned to sets the result's size. If it were the least magnified end of the
        stack, every other frame would have to be shrunk into it, so when the second frame comes out magnified
        relative to the first, Deptheos starts again with the frames in reverse order. The most magnified frame
        then leads, every frame is enlarged into it, and the crop loses nothing. The check is always on, in
        every app and the command line.
      </p>
      <h3>Crop to the area every frame covers</h3>
      <p>
        An aligned frame has no picture along some of its edges, where it moved away. With
        <em>Crop to the area every frame covers</em> on, the default, the result keeps only the area where every
        frame has picture. With it off, the result keeps the full frame, and toward the edges each spot comes
        only from the frames that reach it, so the edges can be less sharp.
      </p>
      <h3>What alignment can't fix</h3>
      <p>
        Alignment moves each frame as a whole. A leg or a leaf that moved on its own, a subject that changed
        shape, or parallax between near and far parts can't be lined up this way; paint those parts in from one
        frame (see <a href="/docs/retouching/">Retouching</a>). Moving the camera on a physical focus rail
        introduces more parallax than stepping focus with the lens's own focus ring, especially at high
        magnification.
      </p>
      <h3>Settings and the command line</h3>
      <p>
        Settings ▸ Alignment holds the grid search's limits (horizontal and vertical
        shift, rotation, magnification; 0 turns rotation or magnification off) and <em>Exposure
        compensation</em>. Leave <em>Exposure compensation</em> off for an ordinary stack: with the exposure
        fixed, it measures the vignetting and the brightness changes of focusing instead, and darkens the
        result. Each render records the limits it was made with. On iPhone and iPad, Settings ▸ Alignment has the
        same four limit sliders and Exposure compensation.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <a href="/docs/cli/">command line</a>, <code>--align ecc</code>, <code>grid</code> or
        <code>none</code> picks the aligner; <code>--grid-shift-x</code>, <code>--grid-shift-y</code>,
        <code>--grid-rotation</code>, <code>--grid-magnification</code> and <code>--grid-brightness</code> (exposure
        compensation) set the grid search; <code>--no-crop</code> turns the crop off.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="moves-section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why frames move</h2>
      <p>
        Moving the focus changes a lens's magnification slightly (focus breathing), so the subject grows or
        shrinks from frame to frame, and a camera on a rail drifts by a fraction of a millimeter. Each step is
        small, but over a whole stack the steps add up to many pixels.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="lab lab-wide" id="moves" data-lab="moves" data-assets="moves">
      <div class="lab-body">
        <img class="lab-poster" src="/media/labs/moves/frame-01.jpg" width="1024" height="683" alt="Frame 1 of the spider stack, as shot" />
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        A jumping spider's 46 frames as shot, beside the same frames aligned: in the aligned ones the spider
        stays still and only the part in focus moves through it. The charts follow the motion ECC found at the
        middle of the frame. The picture shrinks as the focus moves: focus breathing.
      </figcaption>
      <div class="lab-why">
        <p>
          <em>Show what the stack keeps</em> outlines every aligned frame so far, in its frame's color, and
          dashes the area inside all of them: what <em>Crop to the area every frame covers</em> keeps. The
          figure aligns to frame 1; the app leads with the more magnified end of the stack, here the last frame.
        </p>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
